00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:13.930
allow you to use
network attached storage

00:10:13.930 --> 00:10:19.350
or bring in server or local SSD
storage for faster disk I/O.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:21.930
For virtualization,
we spent a lot of time

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.730
saying, how do we get you
consistent performance?

00:10:24.730 --> 00:10:27.240
How do we optimize
performance, and how do we

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:30.600
make it transparent in the way
that your application could

00:10:30.600 --> 00:10:33.280
take best use of
this infrastructure?

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:37.440
So we made a lot of
improvements on vNUMA and NUMA

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:41.882
scheduling to get those
characteristics for you.

00:10:41.882 --> 00:10:43.840
For scale-out workflows,
we spent a lot of time

00:10:43.840 --> 00:10:45.610
on the network stack.

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:49.060
You'll see the A2 family comes
with two flavors, the high GPU

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:51.490
flavor, and the mega GPU flavor.

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:56.968
The high GPU flavor goes
up to 8 GPUs, and 96 vCPUs.

00:10:56.968 --> 00:10:58.510
That's great if you
have a little bit

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:01.540
more pre-processing power
for image recognition

00:11:01.540 --> 00:11:06.840
or other type of workloads
with 12 CPUs for 1 GPU.

00:11:06.840 --> 00:11:11.480
It also comes with 100 gigabits
of network performance.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:14.320
So if you want to scale
out more linearly,

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:18.900
the high GPU is a
nice flavor to have.

00:11:18.900 --> 00:11:22.800
But for the customers who
want the ultimate GPU parallel

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:25.980
performance, we
have A2 mega GPU--

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:32.530
16 GPUs, 100 gigabit networking,
local SSD, high vRAM.

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:35.300
On both of these two flavors
for scale-out workload,

00:11:35.300 --> 00:11:37.580
we spent a lot of
time optimizing

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:39.620
NVIDIA nickel library.

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:41.480
If you just take
the open source,

00:11:41.480 --> 00:11:46.840
out-of-the-box library that
enables VM-to-VM communication

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.540
versus what we have, our
optimized version of that that

00:11:49.540 --> 00:11:52.690
we make available to you-- and
it's something we'll explain

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:54.340
later--

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:58.060
there's a 70% up to
70% speed improvement

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:00.940
for these network tests.

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:02.850
And when you're
scaling your workload

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:07.530
to one VM, two VMs, 10 VMs,
100 VMs and so on and so forth,

00:12:07.530 --> 00:12:11.320
that's really important.

00:12:11.320 --> 00:12:16.011
Let's look a little deeper on
this A2 mega GPU VM instance.

00:12:16.011 --> 00:12:20.560
So NVIDIA has delivered
these HG exports.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:24.110
There's eight GPUs
A100s per board,

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:26.050
and there's a
high-speed NVLink fabric

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:30.190
allowing for GPU-to-GPU
communication on a single HGX.

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:32.450
But we've worked
closely with NVIDIA

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:34.350
to provide a server
platform that

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.550
combines two HGX boards
with a very tightly

00:12:37.550 --> 00:12:40.320
inter-coupled NVLink fabric.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.860
So now you have 16 GPUs
with all-to-all NVLink

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:49.520
connectivity with 9.6 terabytes
per second of peak bandwidth.

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.650
Why that's important is it
allows you a couple of things.

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:57.770
One, it allows you to use
the 640 g of GPU memory

00:12:57.770 --> 00:13:00.500
as a unified memory
fabric through support

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:03.100
of the CUDA tool sets.

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:05.810
Also, what it does, it
enables you to really linearly

00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:07.170
your scale performance.

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:09.460
It's the ultimate
scale-up box because

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:13.010
of the GPU-to-GPU continuity.

00:13:13.010 --> 00:13:15.440
In terms of scale out, again,
I talked about the network

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:17.630
performance where
you can have multiple

00:13:17.630 --> 00:13:19.580
of these systems
running together

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:22.400
that makes [INAUDIBLE]
training go very fast

00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:24.380
or any type of ML
model training.

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:29.390
It also works very well
for a lot of HPC workloads.

00:13:29.390 --> 00:13:32.100
In terms of performance
of one of these VMs,

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:35.170
for FP16, 10
petaFLOPS to reform it

00:13:35.170 --> 00:13:39.110
in a single VM or 20
petaFLOPS for inference.

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:42.110
These terms, these numbers
maybe are meaningless,

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:44.450
maybe they mean something
to you, but what they mean

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:49.843
is model performance
really good TCO.

00:13:49.843 --> 00:13:51.260
