00:21:20.810 --> 00:21:24.320
higher populations.

00:21:24.320 --> 00:21:27.770
And the third major component
of this is our network.

00:21:27.770 --> 00:21:30.210
I've come from a network
background myself,

00:21:30.210 --> 00:21:34.126
and I'm very familiar
with how networks operate.

00:21:34.126 --> 00:21:36.290
And there's two things
that really surprised me

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:39.020
about Google's network
and why it will help here.

00:21:39.020 --> 00:21:41.240
One, just the sheer investment.

00:21:41.240 --> 00:21:43.430
Fiber optic cables,
points of presence,

00:21:43.430 --> 00:21:45.290
software-defined networking.

00:21:45.290 --> 00:21:49.100
The network stack
was built at first

00:21:49.100 --> 00:21:51.680
for ultra-low latency,
that's how do you

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:56.160
get Google search to your web
browser as fast as we can?

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:58.190
We've added a YouTube,
video streaming,

00:21:58.190 --> 00:22:02.930
and a series of other services
for high bandwidth use cases.

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:05.600
That same network that's
optimized for that

00:22:05.600 --> 00:22:07.720
is what powers Google Cloud.

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:10.880
And one feature specifically,
the Google Cloud default

00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:13.460
internet networking
connectivity,

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:16.178
could be called cold potato
routing in the sense of we

00:22:16.178 --> 00:22:17.720
don't want to get
rid of your traffic

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:21.230
and pump it on the internet
as quickly as possible,

00:22:21.230 --> 00:22:23.840
regardless of packet
loss of issues.

00:22:23.840 --> 00:22:26.900
We optimize how do we
do it, low latency,

00:22:26.900 --> 00:22:30.380
how do we connect directly
with ISPs, and let's

00:22:30.380 --> 00:22:33.170
hold it over our own fiber
and our own network as long

00:22:33.170 --> 00:22:35.300
as possible to that end user.

00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:37.160
So the combination
of those things

00:22:37.160 --> 00:22:39.930
means global availability,
low latency on the GPUs,

00:22:39.930 --> 00:22:45.140
a great network, so a very
high quality user experience

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:47.310
for real-time inference work.

00:22:47.310 --> 00:22:49.590
Let's talk about
preemptible GPUs.

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:53.715
In essence, this is our spare
capacity and your cost savings.

00:22:53.715 --> 00:22:56.340
There could be a lot of reasons
why we have spare capacity.

00:22:56.340 --> 00:22:58.770
Maybe we're building ahead
of time before demand,

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:02.160
or maybe other users are
running batch workloads that

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:04.590
spin up thousands of
GPUs at one moment

00:23:04.590 --> 00:23:06.320
and zero GPUs at the other.

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:09.570
But either way, there are
moments we have spare capacity.

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:11.970
So we make it easy to
find that with Kubernetes

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:14.900
Engine or a feature
I'll talk about next.

00:23:14.900 --> 00:23:16.770
But what one
customer did is they

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:20.530
said, how do I use
Kubernetes preemptible GPUs,

00:23:20.530 --> 00:23:23.020
and how do I do
more of the same?

00:23:23.020 --> 00:23:26.450
So they were processing hundreds
of thousands of music files

00:23:26.450 --> 00:23:29.970
and then went to processing
millions of music files, all

00:23:29.970 --> 00:23:31.150
for the same budget.

00:23:31.150 --> 00:23:34.440
It made it easy for them
through preemptible VMs,

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:37.900
and they saved 70% or 80%.

00:23:37.900 --> 00:23:40.780
There is a downside,
which is you can't always

00:23:40.780 --> 00:23:43.400
guarantee this
capacity will be here,

00:23:43.400 --> 00:23:46.300
so you've got to have
flexible completion times.

00:23:46.300 --> 00:23:47.830
In this case, it worked out.

00:23:47.830 --> 00:23:49.570
There's a blog link on our blog.

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:51.520
Please go check it
out, and thank you

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:52.510
for the customer story.

00:23:52.510 --> 00:23:53.600
I really appreciate that.

